# ~/.profile -- run by the init shell at start-up, after /etc/environment, in your home directory.
#
# It is a script: each line runs in the shell itself, as if typed, so assignments, `export`s and `cd`s here are the
# shell's own. A line that fails reports and the rest still run. There is no control flow (no `if`, no `;`).
# Everything below is an example, commented out.
#
# A directory of your own programs on the search path (commands are found there by name, and so are `source`d files):
# PATH=$PATH:$HOME/bin
#
# A different prompt (`\w` is the directory, `\W` its last component, `\$` a `#`):
# PS1='\W> '
#
# Another time zone for `date` and `stat` (exported, so the programs see it):
# export TZ=Europe/London
